# Bounty tracker -- every 3 hours.
#
# The cadence is set by the inventory, not by competition: ~520 standing
# unclaimed bounties that turn over slowly (measured 2026-08-05). Eight runs a
# day sits far inside both documented quotas.
#
# Wall-clock per run is NOT small, and that is deliberate. Search pages are
# paced and a burst throttle is waited out with doubling backoff, because
# abandoning a throttled query costs most of the inventory. A run that takes
# fifteen minutes and returns everything beats one that takes twenty seconds
# and returns page one.
#
# PATH is explicit: cron gets a minimal environment, and node/claude live under
# the nvm prefix (HANDOFF.md trap #8). hunt.py itself needs only system python3
# and requests.
#
# Each job takes its OWN lock, so a job can never overlap ITSELF -- and just as
# importantly, the digest can never block the watch.
#
# They shared one lock for a few hours on 2026-08-10 and that was wrong. A full
# sweep spends ~20 minutes in the timeline stage alone, which is twice the watch
# job's entire interval, so a shared lock starved two to four watch cycles every
# digest. That is the opposite of the priority: the watch is the time-critical
# one. Its whole reason for existing is to catch a label BEFORE it rolls off the
# 100-event page, and that page covered just 27 minutes on Expensify/App when
# measured the same day -- so a starved watch loses labels permanently, while a
# skipped digest merely reports the same inventory three hours later.
#
# They do both write the same SQLite file, which is what the shared lock was
# reaching for. That concern is already handled a layer down: connect() opens
# with timeout=30 and upsert_candidate was hardened for concurrent writers after
# measuring 8 of 10 racing writers collide. Measured
# 2026-08-10: a full sweep of 975 survivors spends ~20 minutes in the timeline
# stage alone at one core call per candidate, which is longer than the watch
# job's entire interval -- so without this the 10-minute watch would start on
# top of a running digest, and both would write the same database. The watch
# sweep is no longer cheap either: since the payout rules landed it clears stale
# rejections and can run paced searches of its own on every invocation.
#
# -n, not -w: a sweep that cannot get the lock should SKIP, not queue. Queueing
# would build a backlog of sweeps racing to report the same inventory, and the
# next scheduled run is only minutes away. Skipping is silent by design here;
# each job's own log records every run it does make, so liveness stays provable.
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/home/ubuntu/.local/bin
DIGEST_LOCK=/tmp/expensify-sniper-digest.lock
WATCH_LOCK=/tmp/expensify-sniper-watch.lock
0 */3 * * * cd $HOME/expensify-sniper && /usr/bin/flock -n $DIGEST_LOCK /usr/bin/python3 hunt.py >> $HOME/expensify-sniper/hunt.log 2>&1

# Payer watch -- every 10 minutes.
#
# A different job from the digest above, because it answers a different
# question. Measured 2026-08-09: proven payers rewarded 501 issues in ninety
# days while holding THREE open unassigned bounties between the ten most recent
# of them. Real bounties are claimed within hours, so a 3-hourly search only
# ever finds what nobody wanted. This catches the label as it lands.
#
# Affordable because it revalidates: /issues/events is core quota (5000/hr) and
# a 304 leaves the quota untouched, so a quiet repo costs nothing. Ten minutes
# is not a preference -- one events page holds 100 entries and covered a single
# DAY on go-gitea/gitea, so a slower sweep loses labels silently.
*/10 * * * * cd $HOME/expensify-sniper && /usr/bin/flock -n $WATCH_LOCK /usr/bin/python3 hunt.py --watch >> $HOME/expensify-sniper/watch.log 2>&1
